.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PDT Tue Sep 29 2026
(This evening through Wednesday night)

Isolated patches of stratus and fog linger along the coast, but the
region remains generally clear. With the marine layer compressing in
response to incoming ridging, any stratus development tonight is
limited to the coastal regions, with fog development possible along
the coast.

A pattern change is underway as ridging over the eastern Pacific
builds into the western United States, with temperatures warming
above to significantly above the seasonal averages (5 to 15 degrees
warmer) as a result. High temperatures today will be in the middle
80s to the middle 90s for the inland valleys, the middle 70s to the
middle 80s near the Bays, and the middle 60s to the lower 70s for
the Pacific coast. Wednesday`s highs will be similar. A breezy
onshore flow should set up each afternoon and evening, while the
overnight and morning period will feature a push of offshore flow
across the interior mountains of the North and East Bay.

Beginning today and especially Wednesday, areas of Moderate HeatRisk
start to pop up across the inland regions. These correspond to a
moderate risk of heat-related illnesses for sensitive populations,
including children, the elderly, pregnant women, people with chronic
illnesses, and people who work or live outdoors without access to
adequate cooling or hydration. These areas are not widespread enough
to put out a Heat Advisory, but still impact several populated
regions, notably the interior valleys of the East Bay and the San
Jose region. Follow these tips to avoid heat exhaustion and
heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

Minor coastal flooding is possible across the bayshore locations of
San Francisco Bay, San Pablo Bay, and Monterey Bay around high tide.
Up to 1.2 feet of inundation above normally dry land is expected,
including up to 0.7 ft of surge caused by thermal expansion and sea
level rise since the sea level datum (basically the point where we
define as "sea level") was set in the 1983-2001 period. At the San
Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.2 ft above
normal (7.01 ft MHHW) at 1:30 PM Tuesday 9/29 and 1.2 ft above
normal (7.03 ft MHHW) at 2:10 PM Wednesday 9/30.

