.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1229 PM PDT Tue Sep 29 2026
(Thursday through next Monday)

Little change in the sensible weather through the week as a rather
resilient ridge sets up across the western United States, supported
by the ensemble model cluster analysis and the ensemble model means
from the European, American, and Canadian ensemble models.
Temperatures may fluctuate a few degrees owing to fluctuations in
the upper level pattern, but for the most part, high temperatures
through the seven day outlook should be similar to today`s highs.
The current forecast even suggests a few degrees of warming for the
upcoming weekend, with warm spots in the interior valleys reaching
the upper 90s and even some isolated triple digits. This is also
when we start to see more widespread areas of Moderate HeatRisk,
although it`s a little early to put out a Heat Advisory. Longer
range outlooks suggest that temperatures above seasonal averages
will continue into the middle of October.

Minor coastal flooding around high tide continues in low-lying areas
across the San Francisco, San Pablo, and Monterey Bay shorelines
through Thursday and potentially into Friday. At the San Francisco
tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 1.1 ft above normal (6.92
ft MHHW) at 2:58 PM on Thursday 10/1.

